; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Factorial, Step by Step
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     recursion, output, debugger, multiplication
; SUMMARY:  Computes factorials with a procedure that outputs a value, and prints how each answer is built from the one below it.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The factorial of 5, written 5!, is 5 x 4 x 3 x 2 x 1 = 120.
;   Notice that 5! is just 5 x 4!. FACT uses that idea: to find the
;   factorial of n it asks for the factorial of n - 1 and multiplies.
;   SHOWFACT does the same thing but prints a line on the way down
;   and another on the way back up, so you can see the two halves.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and read the table and the indented trace.
;   2. Put a breakpoint (click the margin) on the line
;        output :n * fact :n - 1
;      and press F5. Open Call Stack and press F5 a few more times:
;      FACT 5 calls FACT 4 calls FACT 3 ... each one is stuck on this
;      same line, waiting for an answer.
;   3. Now remove that breakpoint and put one on the line
;        if :n = 0 [output 1]
;      Right-click the dot and choose "Stop only when..." and type
;        :n = 0
;      Run again. The stack is at its deepest. Press Step (F11) slowly
;      and watch each FACT get its answer and disappear from the stack.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * OUTPUT hands a value back to whoever called the procedure
;   * the base case (0! is 1) stops the recursion
;   * work done before the recursive call versus after it
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Print fact 20. Then fact 25. Numbers grow very fast.
;   * Write a procedure TRIANGLE :n that adds n + (n-1) + ... + 1 the
;     same way. What should its base case output?
; ============================================================

; 0! is defined as 1. That makes the rule n! = n x (n-1)! work all
; the way down, and gives the recursion somewhere to stop.
to fact :n
  if :n = 0 [output 1]
  output :n * fact :n - 1
end

; The same calculation, but it prints what it is doing. :indent grows
; by two spaces at each level so the printout shows the nesting.
to showfact :n :indent
  localmake "answer 0
  print (word :indent "fact "| | :n "| ?|)
  ifelse :n = 0 [
    make "answer 1
  ] [
    make "answer :n * showfact :n - 1 word :indent "|  |
  ]
  print (word :indent "fact "| | :n "| = | :answer)
  output :answer
end

to table :upto
  for [i 0 :upto] [
    print (sentence (word :i "!) "= fact :i)
  ]
end

print [A table of factorials]
table 10
print []
print [Watch fact 5 go down and come back up:]
print showfact 5 "
